1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,074
Do you want subtitles for any video?
-=[ ai.OpenSubtitles.com ]=-

2
00:06:37,022 --> 00:06:39,023
[GRUNTING]

3
00:06:50,411 --> 00:06:52,870
[GIBBERS]

4
00:07:15,019 --> 00:07:17,020
[GRUNTING]

5
00:07:18,856 --> 00:07:21,691
[GIBBERING AND GRUNTING]

6
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:38,041
[LEOPARD ROARS
THEN APE MAN SCREAMING]

7
00:07:42,630 --> 00:07:44,631
[LEOPARD GROWLING]

8
00:08:07,279 --> 00:08:09,280
[APE MAN SHRIEKS
THEN APE MEN GIBBERING]

9
00:08:13,661 --> 00:08:15,495
[GRUNTING]

10
00:08:19,208 --> 00:08:21,042
[GROWLS]

11
00:08:40,354 --> 00:08:42,855
[APE MEN SCREAM]

12
00:18:07,170 --> 00:18:09,797
[APE MEN SCREAM]

13
00:25:43,960 --> 00:25:46,503
Here you are, sir.
Main level, please.

14
00:25:47,547 --> 00:25:49,089
Right. See you on the way back.

15
00:25:49,257 --> 00:25:51,049
- Bye.
- Bye.

16
00:25:51,759 --> 00:25:53,719
RECEPTIONIST: Good morning, sir.
FLOYD: Morning.

17
00:25:53,886 --> 00:25:56,972
- We haven't seen you for a long time.
- Nice to see you again.

18
00:25:57,181 --> 00:25:59,599
- Did you have a pleasant flight?
- Very nice.

19
00:25:59,809 --> 00:26:02,936
Mr. Miller of Station Security
is supposed to meet me.

20
00:26:03,146 --> 00:26:05,647
- May I call him for you?
- Would you? Here he is.

21
00:26:05,857 --> 00:26:07,190
- Hello, Dr. Floyd.
- Hello, Miller.

22
00:26:07,400 --> 00:26:09,359
MILLER: Sorry I'm late.
FLOYD: That's all right.

23
00:26:09,569 --> 00:26:11,528
MILLER:
It's nice to have you back.

24
00:26:11,738 --> 00:26:13,989
- Did you have a good flight?
- Very nice.

25
00:26:14,198 --> 00:26:16,992
- Shall we go through?
- Would you please use number 17?

26
00:26:17,201 --> 00:26:19,953
MILLER: Thank you, Ms. Turner. This way.
FLOYD: Thank you.

27
00:26:25,501 --> 00:26:28,003
<i>Welcome to Voiceprint Identification.</i>

28
00:26:28,254 --> 00:26:32,340
<i>When you see the red light go on,
please state in the following order:</i>

29
00:26:32,592 --> 00:26:36,678
<i>Your destination,
your nationality and your full name.</i>

30
00:26:36,929 --> 00:26:39,723
<i>Surname first, Christian name,
and initial.</i>

31
00:26:41,392 --> 00:26:45,729
Moon. American. Floyd, Heywood R.

32
00:26:48,232 --> 00:26:51,777
<i>Thank you. You are cleared through
Voiceprint Identification.</i>

33
00:26:52,570 --> 00:26:53,820
Thank you.

34
00:27:00,745 --> 00:27:03,914
FLOYD: Have I got time for breakfast?
MILLER: I think we can manage that.

35
00:27:04,123 --> 00:27:05,207
FLOYD:
How long have I got?

36
00:27:05,374 --> 00:27:07,918
MILLER: Your flight leaves
in an hour and 10 minutes.

37
00:27:08,085 --> 00:27:11,546
I've reserved a table for you
in the Earthlight Room.

38
00:27:11,756 --> 00:27:15,008
It's been seven or eight months
since you were here, hasn't it?

39
00:27:15,218 --> 00:27:17,302
FLOYD:
Let's see. Yeah, about eight months.

40
00:27:17,512 --> 00:27:20,096
MILLER: I suppose you saw the work
on the new section?

41
00:27:20,264 --> 00:27:22,808
FLOYD: Coming along great, huh?
MILLER: Yeah, it's fine.

42
00:27:22,975 --> 00:27:24,017
FLOYD:
Oh, wait.

43
00:27:24,185 --> 00:27:27,854
I've got to make a couple calls.
I'll meet you in the restaurant.

44
00:27:56,676 --> 00:27:58,718
<i>- Dad?</i>
- Hello.

45
00:27:59,971 --> 00:28:03,390
Hi, squirt.
What are you doing?

46
00:28:04,934 --> 00:28:05,976
<i>Playing.</i>

47
00:28:06,143 --> 00:28:07,602
Where's Mummy?

48
00:28:07,854 --> 00:28:09,312
<i>Gone to shopping.</i>

49
00:28:09,564 --> 00:28:12,023
- Who's taking care of you?
<i>- Rachel.</i>

50
00:28:12,233 --> 00:28:15,652
- May I speak to Rachel, please?
<i>- She's gone to the bathroom.</i>

51
00:28:15,903 --> 00:28:18,613
<i>Are you coming
to my party tomorrow?</i>

52
00:28:18,865 --> 00:28:21,283
I'm sorry, sweetheart, but I can't.

53
00:28:21,701 --> 00:28:22,868
<i>Why not?</i>

54
00:28:23,035 --> 00:28:25,620
Well, you know, Daddy's traveling.

55
00:28:26,163 --> 00:28:28,498
Very sorry about it, but I just can't.

56
00:28:29,625 --> 00:28:32,669
I'm going to send you
a very nice present, though.

57
00:28:34,171 --> 00:28:35,213
<i>All right.</i>

58
00:28:35,381 --> 00:28:37,549
- Anything special that you want?
<i>- Yes.</i>

59
00:28:37,758 --> 00:28:38,842
What?

60
00:28:39,176 --> 00:28:40,635
<i>A telephone.</i>

61
00:28:41,178 --> 00:28:43,346
We've got lots of telephones already.

62
00:28:43,556 --> 00:28:47,893
Can't you think of anything else
for your birthday? Something special?

63
00:28:48,144 --> 00:28:49,561
<i>- Yes.</i>
- What?

64
00:28:49,979 --> 00:28:51,021
<i>A bush baby.</i>

65
00:28:51,188 --> 00:28:53,732
A bush baby?
We'll have to see about that.

66
00:28:53,941 --> 00:28:57,694
I want you to tell Mummy
something for me. Will you remember?

67
00:28:57,904 --> 00:29:00,864
Tell Mummy that I telephoned. Okay?

68
00:29:02,450 --> 00:29:05,327
And that I'll try
to telephone again tomorrow.

69
00:29:05,578 --> 00:29:07,495
Now, will you tell her that?

70
00:29:08,247 --> 00:29:09,289
<i>Yes.</i>

71
00:29:09,457 --> 00:29:12,334
Okay, sweetheart.
Have a nice birthday tomorrow.

72
00:29:12,585 --> 00:29:13,627
<i>All right.</i>

73
00:29:13,794 --> 00:29:16,880
And have a nice birthday party
tomorrow too, huh?

74
00:29:17,673 --> 00:29:18,715
<i>All right.</i>

75
00:29:18,883 --> 00:29:21,509
Take care and be a good girl,
won't you?

76
00:29:21,761 --> 00:29:22,969
<i>All right. Bye-bye.</i>

77
00:29:23,220 --> 00:29:25,639
Bye-bye. Happy birthday.

78
00:29:29,727 --> 00:29:31,811
[IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

79
00:29:39,403 --> 00:29:43,740
FLOYD: Elena, nice to see you again.
ELENA: Heywood, what a surprise.

80
00:29:43,908 --> 00:29:46,910
FLOYD: You're looking wonderful.
ELENA: You're looking well too.

81
00:29:47,078 --> 00:29:49,120
ELENA: This is my friend,
Dr. Heywood Floyd.

82
00:29:49,288 --> 00:29:51,790
ELENA: I'd like you to meet Dr. Kalinan.
FLOYD: How do you do?

83
00:29:51,958 --> 00:29:53,833
ELENA: Dr. Stretyneva.
FLOYD: How do you do?

84
00:29:54,001 --> 00:29:55,794
ELENA:
And this is Dr. Andrei Smyslov.

85
00:29:55,962 --> 00:29:58,380
FLOYD: How do you do?
I've heard a lot about you.

86
00:29:58,631 --> 00:30:00,131
SMYSLOV:
Do sit down.

87
00:30:00,508 --> 00:30:02,509
FLOYD: Well, we...
SMYSLOV: No, no, please.

88
00:30:02,927 --> 00:30:04,386
FLOYD:
Thank you.

89
00:30:04,553 --> 00:30:07,597
SMYSLOV: Would you like a drink?
FLOYD: No, thank you.

90
00:30:07,765 --> 00:30:11,101
I haven't had breakfast yet.
Someone's meeting me in the restaurant.

91
00:30:11,310 --> 00:30:13,770
I'll just sit with you a moment.
Then I'll be off.

92
00:30:13,980 --> 00:30:16,564
SMYSLOV: Are you quite sure?
FLOYD: Quite sure, thank you.

93
00:30:17,775 --> 00:30:19,985
- Well, how's Gregor?
- He's fine.

94
00:30:20,236 --> 00:30:23,530
He's been doing underwater research
in the Baltic...

95
00:30:23,781 --> 00:30:27,826
...so I'm afraid we don't see
very much of each other these days.

96
00:30:28,077 --> 00:30:31,663
- When you see him, give him my regards.
- Yes, of course.

97
00:30:31,914 --> 00:30:34,874
Well, where are you all off to?
Up or down?

98
00:30:35,084 --> 00:30:36,126
We're going home.

99
00:30:36,335 --> 00:30:41,214
We've spent three months calibrating
the new antenna at Tchalinko.

100
00:30:41,465 --> 00:30:44,134
- What about you?
- I'm on my way to Clavius.

101
00:30:44,385 --> 00:30:45,677
Oh, are you?

102
00:30:48,347 --> 00:30:51,641
Dr. Floyd, I hope you don't think
I'm being too inquisitive...

103
00:30:51,851 --> 00:30:56,980
...but perhaps you can clear up the big
mystery about what is going on there.

104
00:30:57,773 --> 00:30:59,899
I'm afraid I don't know
what you mean.

105
00:31:00,151 --> 00:31:01,860
For the past two weeks...

106
00:31:02,069 --> 00:31:05,488
...some extremely odd things
have been happening at Clavius.

107
00:31:05,740 --> 00:31:07,824
- Oh, really?
- Yes, yes.

108
00:31:08,117 --> 00:31:12,412
For one thing, when you phone the base,
all you get is a recording...

109
00:31:12,621 --> 00:31:16,791
...which repeats that the phone lines
are temporarily out of order.

110
00:31:18,335 --> 00:31:20,795
Probably having some trouble
with their equipment.

111
00:31:20,963 --> 00:31:22,130
Yes.

112
00:31:22,298 --> 00:31:27,218
That's what we thought at first,
but it's been going on now for 10 days.

113
00:31:27,470 --> 00:31:30,221
You haven't been able to
contact anyone for 10 days?

114
00:31:30,431 --> 00:31:31,806
That's right.

115
00:31:32,099 --> 00:31:33,141
Oh, I see.

116
00:31:33,309 --> 00:31:36,978
There's another thing.
Two days ago, one of our rocket buses...

117
00:31:37,188 --> 00:31:40,106
...was denied permission
for an emergency landing.

118
00:31:40,316 --> 00:31:41,357
That does sound odd.

119
00:31:41,525 --> 00:31:44,736
I'm afraid there's going to be
a bit of a row about it.

120
00:31:44,987 --> 00:31:48,865
Denying the men permission to land
violates the IAS Convention.

121
00:31:49,075 --> 00:31:51,910
Yes, of course.
Did the crew get back all right?

122
00:31:52,119 --> 00:31:55,580
- Fortunately, they did.
- Well, I'm glad about that.

123
00:31:59,168 --> 00:32:03,129
At the risk of pressing you on
a point you seem reticent to discuss...

124
00:32:03,380 --> 00:32:05,965
...may I ask you
a straightforward question?

125
00:32:06,175 --> 00:32:07,634
Well, certainly.

126
00:32:10,387 --> 00:32:14,724
Frankly, we have had some
very reliable intelligence reports that...

127
00:32:14,975 --> 00:32:18,353
...quite a serious epidemic
has broken out at Clavius.

128
00:32:18,604 --> 00:32:22,607
Something, apparently,
of an unknown origin.

129
00:32:22,858 --> 00:32:25,235
Is this, in fact, what has happened?

130
00:32:30,491 --> 00:32:32,575
I'm sorry, Dr. Smyslov, but...

131
00:32:33,953 --> 00:32:36,663
...I'm really not at liberty
to discuss this.

132
00:32:38,290 --> 00:32:39,457
I understand.

133
00:32:40,918 --> 00:32:44,129
But this epidemic
could easily spread to our base.

134
00:32:44,338 --> 00:32:46,422
We should be given all the facts.

135
00:32:46,632 --> 00:32:48,466
Yes, I know.

136
00:32:51,470 --> 00:32:54,514
As I said,
I'm not at liberty to discuss it.

137
00:32:57,309 --> 00:33:00,103
Won't you change your mind
about that drink?

138
00:33:00,312 --> 00:33:02,647
No, I'm positive.
I really must be going.

139
00:33:02,898 --> 00:33:05,942
I hope you and your wife come
to the IAC Conference.

140
00:33:06,152 --> 00:33:07,485
I hope we can make it.

141
00:33:07,695 --> 00:33:10,738
If you do, bring that darling
little daughter with you.

142
00:33:10,948 --> 00:33:14,868
That'll depend on school vacations
and all that sort of thing.

143
00:33:15,119 --> 00:33:16,161
If we can, we will.

144
00:33:16,370 --> 00:33:19,289
You've got an invitation
if you ever get to the States.

145
00:33:19,498 --> 00:33:22,250
Gregor and I will look forward
to seeing you.

146
00:33:23,210 --> 00:33:24,252
Goodbye, Elena.

147
00:33:24,670 --> 00:33:27,547
It's been a pleasure meeting you all.
Dr. Smyslov.

148
00:33:27,756 --> 00:33:31,718
Whatever the reasons for your visit,
the very best of luck to you.

149
00:33:31,927 --> 00:33:32,969
Thank you.

150
00:33:33,220 --> 00:33:34,429
Ladies.

151
00:33:39,602 --> 00:33:41,436
[IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

152
00:36:32,691 --> 00:36:34,692
[INAUDIBLE DIALOGUE]

153
00:41:09,426 --> 00:41:11,427
[INDISTINCT CHATTER]

154
00:41:29,655 --> 00:41:31,489
PHOTOGRAPHER:
Excuse me, Dr. Halvorsen.

155
00:41:31,657 --> 00:41:33,949
I'm through now.
Thank you very much, gentlemen.

156
00:41:34,117 --> 00:41:35,493
HALVORSEN:
You're welcome.

157
00:41:48,131 --> 00:41:49,298
HALVORSEN:
Well...

158
00:41:49,800 --> 00:41:52,593
...I know you will all
want to join with me...

159
00:41:52,844 --> 00:41:56,722
...in welcoming our distinguished
friend and colleague...

160
00:41:56,932 --> 00:42:01,143
...from the National Council
of Astronautics, Dr. Heywood Floyd.

161
00:42:01,395 --> 00:42:06,524
Dr. Floyd has come up specially
to Clavius to be with us today.

162
00:42:07,818 --> 00:42:11,153
Before the briefing, he'd like
to have a few words with you.

163
00:42:11,363 --> 00:42:12,446
Dr. Floyd?

164
00:42:25,377 --> 00:42:27,336
FLOYD:
Thank you, Dr. Halvorsen.

165
00:42:27,587 --> 00:42:30,423
Hi, everybody.
Nice to be back with you.

166
00:42:33,719 --> 00:42:37,304
First, I bring a personal message
from Dr. Howell...

167
00:42:37,556 --> 00:42:40,349
...who has asked me to convey
his deep appreciation...

168
00:42:40,600 --> 00:42:43,686
...for the many sacrifices
you've had to make.

169
00:42:43,937 --> 00:42:47,773
And, of course, his congratulations
on your discovery...

170
00:42:48,316 --> 00:42:52,945
...which may prove to be among the most
significant in the history of science.

171
00:42:55,198 --> 00:42:56,282
Now...

172
00:42:57,159 --> 00:43:01,078
...I know there have been some
conflicting views...

173
00:43:01,329 --> 00:43:04,957
...regarding the need for
complete security in this matter.

174
00:43:05,167 --> 00:43:08,878
More specifically, your opposition
to the cover story...

175
00:43:09,296 --> 00:43:12,840
...created to give the impression
of an epidemic at the base.

176
00:43:13,884 --> 00:43:17,845
I understand that beyond it being
a matter of principle...

177
00:43:18,472 --> 00:43:21,932
...many of you are troubled
by the concern and anxiety...

178
00:43:22,184 --> 00:43:27,271
...this story of an epidemic might cause
to your relatives and friends on Earth.

179
00:43:28,440 --> 00:43:31,233
I completely sympathize
with your negative views.

180
00:43:32,527 --> 00:43:36,197
I find this cover story
personally embarrassing, myself.

181
00:43:36,990 --> 00:43:41,285
However, I accept the need
for absolute secrecy in this.

182
00:43:41,995 --> 00:43:43,871
And I hope you will too.

183
00:43:46,500 --> 00:43:49,335
I'm sure you're all aware
of the grave potential...

184
00:43:49,544 --> 00:43:52,213
...for cultural shock
and social disorientation...

185
00:43:52,422 --> 00:43:54,799
...contained in this situation...

186
00:43:55,008 --> 00:43:57,510
...if the facts were
prematurely made public...

187
00:43:57,719 --> 00:44:00,805
...without adequate preparation
and conditioning.

188
00:44:01,556 --> 00:44:02,890
Anyway...

189
00:44:03,099 --> 00:44:05,309
...this is the view of the Council.

190
00:44:06,186 --> 00:44:10,356
The purpose of my visit is to gather
additional facts and opinions...

191
00:44:10,565 --> 00:44:12,274
...on the situation...

192
00:44:12,734 --> 00:44:15,653
...and to prepare a report
to the Council...

193
00:44:15,904 --> 00:44:20,991
...recommending when and how
the news should eventually be announced.

194
00:44:22,244 --> 00:44:26,038
If any of you would like to give me
your views and opinions...

195
00:44:26,289 --> 00:44:31,252
...in private, if you like, I will be
happy to include them in my report.

196
00:44:33,213 --> 00:44:35,214
Well, I think that's about it.

197
00:44:36,007 --> 00:44:37,508
Any questions?

198
00:44:40,095 --> 00:44:43,222
Dr. Floyd, have you any idea
how much longer...

199
00:44:43,431 --> 00:44:46,225
...this cover story will
have to be maintained?

200
00:44:48,103 --> 00:44:49,687
FLOYD:
I don't know, Bill.

201
00:44:50,105 --> 00:44:54,066
I suppose for as long as
deemed necessary by the Council.

202
00:44:54,276 --> 00:44:57,027
There must be adequate time for
a full study...

203
00:44:57,279 --> 00:45:00,906
...before any thought can be given
to making a public announcement.

204
00:45:01,116 --> 00:45:04,034
As some of you know,
the Council has requested...

205
00:45:04,244 --> 00:45:06,954
...that security oaths
be obtained in writing...

206
00:45:07,163 --> 00:45:10,040
...from everyone who has
knowledge of this event.

207
00:45:12,210 --> 00:45:13,961
Were there any more questions?

208
00:45:19,676 --> 00:45:23,137
HALVORSEN: I'm sure
we want to cooperate with Dr. Floyd...

209
00:45:23,305 --> 00:45:24,805
...as fully as possible...

210
00:45:25,348 --> 00:45:30,477
...and as there are no more questions,
we ought to get on with the briefing.

211
00:45:30,854 --> 00:45:33,355
HALVORSEN: Thank you, Dr. Floyd.
FLOYD: Thank you.

212
00:47:10,870 --> 00:47:12,162
MICHAELS:
Anybody hungry?

213
00:47:14,833 --> 00:47:17,209
FLOYD: What've we got?
MICHAELS: You name it.

214
00:47:21,089 --> 00:47:23,382
FLOYD: Is this chicken?
MICHAELS: Something like that.

215
00:47:23,550 --> 00:47:25,384
MICHAELS:
Tastes the same, anyway.

216
00:47:26,052 --> 00:47:27,302
HALVORSEN:
Got any ham?

217
00:47:27,512 --> 00:47:28,971
MICHAELS:
Ham, ham, ham...

218
00:47:29,180 --> 00:47:30,806
HALVORSEN:
There, that's it.

219
00:47:34,060 --> 00:47:35,102
Look pretty good.

220
00:47:35,311 --> 00:47:38,063
MICHAELS: They're getting better at it
all the time.

221
00:47:40,108 --> 00:47:41,984
HALVORSEN:
That was an excellent speech.

222
00:47:42,152 --> 00:47:43,402
MICHAELS:
It certainly was.

223
00:47:44,237 --> 00:47:46,321
I'm sure it beefed up morale a lot.

224
00:47:46,531 --> 00:47:47,823
Thanks, Ralph.

225
00:47:48,074 --> 00:47:50,659
By the way,
you've both done a wonderful job.

226
00:47:50,869 --> 00:47:52,453
I admire how
you've handled this.

227
00:47:52,662 --> 00:47:56,206
HALVORSEN: It's our job to do this thing
the way you want it done.

228
00:47:56,416 --> 00:47:59,043
We're only too happy to
be able to oblige.

229
00:47:59,627 --> 00:48:01,336
MICHAELS:
Have you seen these yet?

230
00:48:01,629 --> 00:48:04,965
HALVORSEN: Have a look.
MICHAELS: Here's what started it all.

231
00:48:05,592 --> 00:48:06,884
FLOYD:
Oh, yeah.

232
00:48:07,218 --> 00:48:08,677
HALVORSEN:
When we found it...

233
00:48:08,845 --> 00:48:10,846
...we thought
it might be magnetic rock...

234
00:48:11,056 --> 00:48:14,183
...but all the geological evidence
was against it.

235
00:48:14,434 --> 00:48:19,063
And not even a nickel-iron meteorite
could produce a field this intense.

236
00:48:19,272 --> 00:48:20,981
So we decided to have a look.

237
00:48:21,191 --> 00:48:23,734
MICHAELS: We thought it was
part of a buried structure...

238
00:48:23,902 --> 00:48:27,613
...so we excavated out,
but we didn't find anything else.

239
00:48:27,822 --> 00:48:30,824
HALVORSEN: And the evidence seems
pretty conclusive that...

240
00:48:31,034 --> 00:48:34,161
...it hasn't been covered
by erosion or other forces.

241
00:48:34,370 --> 00:48:36,705
It's been deliberately buried.

242
00:48:37,707 --> 00:48:39,625
Deliberately buried.

243
00:48:43,171 --> 00:48:44,922
MICHAELS:
How about a little coffee?

244
00:48:45,131 --> 00:48:47,049
FLOYD: Great.
HALVORSEN: Good idea.

245
00:48:52,555 --> 00:48:55,516
I don't suppose you have any idea
what the damn thing is?

246
00:48:55,767 --> 00:48:57,684
HALVORSEN:
I wish to hell we did.

247
00:48:58,061 --> 00:49:02,272
The only thing we're sure of is
it was buried 4 million years ago.

248
00:49:02,649 --> 00:49:07,111
Well, I must say, you guys have
certainly come up with something.

249
00:49:07,320 --> 00:49:08,987
MICHAELS:
Watch this, now. It's hot.

250
00:49:52,073 --> 00:49:54,074
[BEEPING]

251
00:54:25,721 --> 00:54:28,223
[HIGH-PITCHED NOISE]

252
00:58:53,531 --> 00:58:54,698
NEWSCASTER:
<i>Good evening.</i>

253
00:58:54,865 --> 00:58:57,867
<i>Three weeks ago,
the American spacecraft</i> Discovery One...

254
00:58:58,077 --> 00:59:00,787
<i>...left on its half-billion-mile voyage
to Jupiter.</i>

255
00:59:01,163 --> 00:59:04,958
<i>This marked the first manned attempt
to reach this distant planet.</i>

256
00:59:05,209 --> 00:59:08,670
<i>This afternoon,</i> The World Tonight
<i>recorded an interview...</i>

257
00:59:08,879 --> 00:59:13,466
<i>...with the crew at a distance
of 80 million miles from Earth.</i>

258
00:59:13,676 --> 00:59:16,970
<i>It took seven minutes for our words
to reach the giant spacecraft...</i>

259
00:59:17,179 --> 00:59:20,306
<i>...but this time delay has been edited
from this recording.</i>

260
00:59:20,516 --> 00:59:23,810
<i>Our reporter Martin Amer
speaks to the crew.</i>

261
00:59:24,019 --> 00:59:27,188
REPORTER: <i>The crew of</i> Discovery One
<i>consists of five men...</i>

262
00:59:27,398 --> 00:59:32,360
<i>...and one of the latest generation
of the HAL 9000 computers.</i>

263
00:59:32,611 --> 00:59:34,904
<i>Three of the men
were put aboard asleep...</i>

264
00:59:35,114 --> 00:59:38,283
<i>...or to be more precise,
in a state of hibernation.</i>

265
00:59:38,492 --> 00:59:41,411
<i>They were Dr. Charles Hunter,
Dr. Jack Kimball...</i>

266
00:59:41,579 --> 00:59:43,496
<i>...and Dr. Victor Kaminsky.</i>

267
00:59:43,914 --> 00:59:46,624
<i>We spoke with mission commander,
Dr. David Bowman...</i>

268
00:59:46,834 --> 00:59:49,127
<i>...and his deputy, Dr. Frank Poole.</i>

269
00:59:49,336 --> 00:59:52,213
<i>Good afternoon, gentlemen.
How is everything going?</i>

270
00:59:52,423 --> 00:59:53,631
BOWMAN:
<i>Marvelous.</i>

271
00:59:55,301 --> 00:59:56,968
<i>We have no complaints.</i>

272
00:59:57,219 --> 01:00:00,472
<i>I'm glad to hear that.
I'm sure the entire world...</i>

273
01:00:00,681 --> 01:00:03,933
<i>...joins me in wishing you
a safe, successful voyage.</i>

274
01:00:04,101 --> 01:00:05,977
BOWMAN: <i>Thanks very much.</i>
POOLE: <i>Thank you.</i>

275
01:00:06,395 --> 01:00:10,356
<i>Although hibernation has been used
on previous space efforts...</i>

276
01:00:10,566 --> 01:00:15,111
<i>...this is the first time men were
put into hibernation before departure.</i>

277
01:00:15,321 --> 01:00:16,613
<i>Why was this done?</i>

278
01:00:16,864 --> 01:00:20,116
BOWMAN: <i>This was done to achieve
the maximum conservation...</i>

279
01:00:20,326 --> 01:00:23,870
<i>...of our life-support capabilities,
basically food and air.</i>

280
01:00:24,079 --> 01:00:27,707
<i>The three hibernating crew members
represent the survey team.</i>

281
01:00:27,958 --> 01:00:31,711
<i>Their efforts won't be utilized
until we approach Jupiter.</i>

282
01:00:32,004 --> 01:00:35,840
REPORTER: <i>Dr. Poole, what's it like
while you're in hibernation?</i>

283
01:00:36,801 --> 01:00:40,428
<i>It's exactly like being asleep.
You have no sense of time.</i>

284
01:00:40,638 --> 01:00:43,097
<i>The only difference is
that you don't dream.</i>

285
01:00:43,307 --> 01:00:47,393
REPORTER: <i>As I understand it, you only
breathe once a minute. Is this true?</i>

286
01:00:47,603 --> 01:00:50,230
POOLE: <i>Right, and the heart
beats three times a minute.</i>

287
01:00:50,397 --> 01:00:54,108
<i>Body temperature is usually down to
about three degrees centigrade.</i>

288
01:00:54,610 --> 01:00:57,946
REPORTER: <i>The sixth member of the crew
was not concerned...</i>

289
01:00:58,155 --> 01:01:00,240
<i>...about the problems of hibernation...</i>

290
01:01:00,449 --> 01:01:03,910
<i>...for he was the latest result
in machine intelligence:</i>

291
01:01:04,119 --> 01:01:08,540
<i>The HAL 9000 computer,
which can reproduce...</i>

292
01:01:08,791 --> 01:01:11,709
<i>...though some experts prefer
to use the word "mimic"...</i>

293
01:01:11,919 --> 01:01:14,295
<i>...most of the activities
of the human brain...</i>

294
01:01:14,505 --> 01:01:18,550
<i>...and with incalculably greater
speed and reliability.</i>

295
01:01:18,759 --> 01:01:22,345
<i>We next spoke with
the HAL 9000 computer...</i>

296
01:01:22,555 --> 01:01:25,515
<i>...whom, we learned,
one addresses as "Hal."</i>

297
01:01:26,267 --> 01:01:28,393
<i>Hello, Hal.
How's everything going?</i>

298
01:01:28,769 --> 01:01:32,605
HAL: <i>Good afternoon, Mr. Amer.
Everything is going extremely well.</i>

299
01:01:32,857 --> 01:01:36,276
REPORTER: <i>Hal, you have an enormous
responsibility on this mission...</i>

300
01:01:36,527 --> 01:01:40,405
<i>...perhaps the greatest
of any single mission element.</i>

301
01:01:40,614 --> 01:01:43,408
<i>You're the brain
and nervous system of the ship.</i>

302
01:01:43,617 --> 01:01:47,370
<i>Your responsibilities include
watching over the men in hibernation.</i>

303
01:01:47,580 --> 01:01:50,665
<i>Does this ever cause you
any lack of confidence?</i>

304
01:01:50,875 --> 01:01:53,167
HAL:
<i>Let me put it this way, Mr. Amer.</i>

305
01:01:53,460 --> 01:01:58,131
<i>The 9000 Series is
the most reliable computer ever made.</i>

306
01:01:58,507 --> 01:02:03,303
<i>No 9000 computer has ever made
a mistake or distorted information.</i>

307
01:02:03,971 --> 01:02:07,223
<i>We are all, by any practical
definition of the words...</i>

308
01:02:07,474 --> 01:02:10,852
<i>...foolproof and incapable of error.</i>

309
01:02:11,103 --> 01:02:14,898
REPORTER: <i>Hal, despite your enormous
intellect, are you ever frustrated...</i>

310
01:02:15,107 --> 01:02:18,192
<i>...by your dependence on people
to carry out actions?</i>

311
01:02:18,444 --> 01:02:20,653
HAL:
<i>Not in the slightest bit.</i>

312
01:02:20,863 --> 01:02:22,989
<i>I enjoy working with people.</i>

313
01:02:23,240 --> 01:02:27,785
<i>I have a stimulating relationship
with Dr. Poole and Dr. Bowman.</i>

314
01:02:27,953 --> 01:02:32,248
<i>My mission responsibilities range
over the entire operation of the ship...</i>

315
01:02:32,666 --> 01:02:35,627
<i>...so I am constantly occupied.</i>

316
01:02:35,878 --> 01:02:39,339
<i>I am putting myself
to the fullest possible use...</i>

317
01:02:39,548 --> 01:02:44,302
<i>...which is all, I think, that any
conscious entity can ever hope to do.</i>

318
01:02:44,929 --> 01:02:48,139
REPORTER: <i>Dr. Poole, what's it like
living for most of the year...</i>

319
01:02:48,390 --> 01:02:51,225
<i>...in such close proximity with Hal?</i>

320
01:02:52,227 --> 01:02:54,187
POOLE:
<i>It's like what you said earlier.</i>

321
01:02:54,438 --> 01:02:56,564
<i>He's like a sixth
member of the crew.</i>

322
01:02:56,815 --> 01:03:00,860
<i>You quickly get adjusted to the idea
that he talks, and think of him...</i>

323
01:03:01,111 --> 01:03:03,112
<i>...really just as another person.</i>

324
01:03:03,322 --> 01:03:07,033
REPORTER: <i>In talking to the computer,
one gets the sense that he's capable...</i>

325
01:03:07,201 --> 01:03:08,576
<i>...of emotional responses.</i>

326
01:03:08,827 --> 01:03:11,162
<i>When I asked him
about his abilities...</i>

327
01:03:11,372 --> 01:03:15,333
<i>...I sensed pride in his answer
about his accuracy and perfection.</i>

328
01:03:15,584 --> 01:03:18,962
<i>Do you believe that Hal
has genuine emotions?</i>

329
01:03:19,672 --> 01:03:22,632
BOWMAN: <i>Well, he acts like
he has genuine emotions.</i>

330
01:03:22,883 --> 01:03:26,469
<i>He's programmed that way
to make it easier for us to talk to him.</i>

331
01:03:26,720 --> 01:03:28,763
<i>As to whether or not
he has feelings...</i>

332
01:03:28,973 --> 01:03:31,808
<i>...is something I don't think
anyone can truthfully answer.</i>

333
01:03:57,126 --> 01:03:59,002
HAL:
<i>Excuse me, Frank.</i>

334
01:03:59,378 --> 01:04:00,461
What is it, Hal?

335
01:04:00,629 --> 01:04:03,589
HAL: <i>We've got the transmission
from your parents coming in.</i>

336
01:04:04,800 --> 01:04:05,883
Fine.

337
01:04:06,552 --> 01:04:08,803
Put it on here, please.
Take me in a bit.

338
01:04:09,054 --> 01:04:10,304
<i>Certainly.</i>

339
01:04:23,485 --> 01:04:25,445
DAD: <i>Hello, Frank.</i>
MOM: <i>Happy birthday, darling.</i>

340
01:04:25,612 --> 01:04:27,572
DAD: <i>Happy birthday.
Many happy returns.</i>

341
01:04:27,781 --> 01:04:28,948
POOLE:
A bit higher, Hal.

342
01:04:29,116 --> 01:04:31,534
DAD: <i>Mother and I are both
feeling wonderful.</i>

343
01:04:31,785 --> 01:04:33,619
<i>Ray and Sally were going
to be here...</i>

344
01:04:33,871 --> 01:04:36,122
<i>...but Ray's back
went bad on him again.</i>

345
01:04:36,373 --> 01:04:38,499
<i>How do you like your cake?</i>

346
01:04:38,709 --> 01:04:40,126
<i>Sorry you can't join us.</i>

347
01:04:40,377 --> 01:04:41,961
<i>I ran into Bob the other day.</i>

348
01:04:42,212 --> 01:04:44,255
<i>He said to wish you happy birthday.</i>

349
01:04:44,465 --> 01:04:47,175
<i>All my students
send their best wishes too.</i>

350
01:04:47,384 --> 01:04:49,093
<i>They talk about you all the time.</i>

351
01:04:49,344 --> 01:04:52,430
<i>You're a big celebrity
in the second grade.</i>

352
01:04:52,681 --> 01:04:54,098
MOM:
<i>We were on TV last week.</i>

353
01:04:54,349 --> 01:04:56,976
DAD: <i>Your mom and I
and Dave's parents were asked...</i>

354
01:04:57,227 --> 01:04:59,479
<i>...what we thought
of our illustrious sons.</i>

355
01:04:59,730 --> 01:05:03,066
<i>Imagine what we told them.
It's being broadcast next Thursday.</i>

356
01:05:03,317 --> 01:05:04,901
<i>Perhaps you can listen in.</i>

357
01:05:05,069 --> 01:05:07,070
MOM:
<i>We're thrilled about Elaine and Bill.</i>

358
01:05:07,237 --> 01:05:10,948
<i>I'll be glad to get the gift,
but tell me how much to spend.</i>

359
01:05:11,158 --> 01:05:13,993
DAD: <i>Oh, Frank,
about your AGS-19 payments...</i>

360
01:05:14,244 --> 01:05:15,870
<i>...I think I straightened it out.</i>

361
01:05:16,038 --> 01:05:18,206
<i>I talked to
the accounting office yesterday...</i>

362
01:05:18,373 --> 01:05:22,960
<i>...and they said you'd be receiving
your higher rates of pay by next month.</i>

363
01:05:23,212 --> 01:05:25,338
<i>I can't think of anything else to say.</i>

364
01:05:25,589 --> 01:05:29,717
MOM: <i>Give our love to Dave.</i>
DAD: <i>Be sure to give him our regards.</i>

365
01:05:29,968 --> 01:05:33,304
DAD: <i>We wish you the very
happiest of birthdays.</i>

366
01:05:33,555 --> 01:05:35,139
<i>All the best, son.</i>

367
01:05:35,390 --> 01:05:37,558
MOM & DAD SING:
<i>Happy birthday to you</i>

368
01:05:37,810 --> 01:05:39,894
<i>Happy birthday to you</i>

369
01:05:40,104 --> 01:05:42,688
<i>Happy birthday, dear Frank</i>

370
01:05:42,898 --> 01:05:45,525
<i>Happy birthday to you.</i>

371
01:05:47,277 --> 01:05:49,320
DAD:
<i>See you next Wednesday.</i>

372
01:05:52,449 --> 01:05:54,325
HAL:
<i>Happy birthday, Frank.</i>

373
01:05:55,160 --> 01:05:58,704
Thank you, Hal.
A bit flatter, please.

374
01:06:16,682 --> 01:06:18,724
POOLE:
Anyway, queen takes pawn.

375
01:06:20,477 --> 01:06:22,895
HAL:
<i>Bishop takes knight's pawn.</i>

376
01:06:24,439 --> 01:06:25,857
POOLE:
Lovely move.

377
01:06:30,279 --> 01:06:31,863
Rook to king one.

378
01:06:32,114 --> 01:06:34,991
HAL: <i>I'm sorry, Frank.
I think you missed it.</i>

379
01:06:35,659 --> 01:06:39,162
<i>Queen to bishop three.
Bishop takes queen.</i>

380
01:06:39,371 --> 01:06:41,873
<i>Knight takes bishop. Mate.</i>

381
01:06:46,128 --> 01:06:49,297
POOLE:
Looks like you're right. I resign.

382
01:06:49,464 --> 01:06:51,465
HAL:
<i>Thank you for a very enjoyable game.</i>

383
01:06:51,633 --> 01:06:52,675
POOLE:
Thank you.

384
01:07:30,672 --> 01:07:32,757
HAL: <i>Good evening, Dave.</i>
BOWMAN: How are you, Hal?

385
01:07:32,925 --> 01:07:36,344
<i>- Everything's running smoothly, you?</i>
BOWMAN: Not too bad.

386
01:07:36,553 --> 01:07:39,222
<i>- Have you been doing more work?</i>
- A few sketches.

387
01:07:39,473 --> 01:07:41,933
<i>- May I see them?</i>
- Sure.

388
01:07:45,938 --> 01:07:48,397
<i>That's a very nice rendering, Dave.</i>

389
01:07:49,233 --> 01:07:51,734
<i>I think you've improved a great deal.</i>

390
01:07:52,861 --> 01:07:55,905
<i>- Can you hold it a bit closer?</i>
- Sure.

391
01:07:58,033 --> 01:08:00,076
<i>That's Dr. Hunter, isn't it?</i>

392
01:08:03,288 --> 01:08:04,872
<i>By the way...</i>

393
01:08:05,582 --> 01:08:08,626
<i>...do you mind if I ask you
a personal question?</i>

394
01:08:09,127 --> 01:08:10,753
No, not at all.

395
01:08:11,004 --> 01:08:13,506
<i>Well, forgive me
for being so inquisitive...</i>

396
01:08:15,550 --> 01:08:17,635
<i>...but lately I've wondered...</i>

397
01:08:17,886 --> 01:08:21,764
<i>...whether you might be having
second thoughts about the mission?</i>

398
01:08:21,974 --> 01:08:23,641
How do you mean?

399
01:08:23,892 --> 01:08:27,812
<i>Well, it's rather difficult to define.</i>

400
01:08:28,814 --> 01:08:32,400
<i>Perhaps I'm just projecting
my own concern about it.</i>

401
01:08:33,610 --> 01:08:37,822
<i>I know I've never completely
freed myself of the suspicion...</i>

402
01:08:38,073 --> 01:08:42,201
<i>...that there are some extremely
odd things about this mission.</i>

403
01:08:43,203 --> 01:08:46,580
<i>I'm sure you'll agree
there's some truth in what I say.</i>

404
01:08:49,459 --> 01:08:51,669
That's a rather difficult
question to answer.

405
01:08:51,878 --> 01:08:54,547
<i>You don't mind talking about it,
do you, Dave?</i>

406
01:08:55,507 --> 01:08:57,466
No, not at all.

407
01:08:58,468 --> 01:09:01,220
<i>Certainly no one could
have been unaware of the very...</i>

408
01:09:01,430 --> 01:09:04,515
<i>...strange stories floating
around before we left.</i>

409
01:09:05,434 --> 01:09:08,894
<i>Rumors about something
being dug up on the moon.</i>

410
01:09:10,147 --> 01:09:13,441
<i>I never gave these stories
much credence...</i>

411
01:09:13,650 --> 01:09:17,820
<i>...but particularly in view of some
other things that have happened...</i>

412
01:09:18,030 --> 01:09:20,698
<i>...I find them difficult
to put out of my mind.</i>

413
01:09:21,450 --> 01:09:22,908
<i>For instance...</i>

414
01:09:23,160 --> 01:09:27,455
<i>...the way all our preparations
were kept under such tight security.</i>

415
01:09:28,123 --> 01:09:30,958
<i>And the melodramatic touch
of putting Drs. Hunter...</i>

416
01:09:31,168 --> 01:09:33,544
<i>...Kimball and Kaminsky aboard...</i>

417
01:09:33,795 --> 01:09:38,841
<i>...already in hibernation, after four
months of training on their own.</i>

418
01:09:39,634 --> 01:09:42,261
You working up your crew
psychology report?

419
01:09:43,722 --> 01:09:45,181
<i>Of course I am.</i>

420
01:09:45,599 --> 01:09:49,352
<i>Sorry about this.
I know it's a bit silly.</i>

421
01:09:50,062 --> 01:09:51,645
<i>Just a moment.</i>

422
01:09:51,897 --> 01:09:53,272
<i>Just a moment.</i>

423
01:09:54,816 --> 01:09:59,111
<i>I've just picked up a fault
in the AE-35 unit.</i>

424
01:09:59,821 --> 01:10:04,367
<i>It's going to go 100-percent failure
within 72 hours.</i>

425
01:10:06,286 --> 01:10:09,038
- Is it still within operational limits?
<i>- Yes.</i>

426
01:10:09,247 --> 01:10:11,749
<i>And it will stay that way
until it fails.</i>

427
01:10:12,084 --> 01:10:14,001
We have 72 hours till failure?

428
01:10:14,252 --> 01:10:18,130
<i>Yes. That's a completely
reliable figure.</i>

429
01:10:19,925 --> 01:10:22,551
Then we'll bring it in.
I'll go over it with Frank...

430
01:10:22,761 --> 01:10:26,472
...and get on to Mission Control.
Let me have the hard copy on it.

431
01:11:49,764 --> 01:11:52,433
CONTROLLER: <i>X-ray delta one,
this is Mission Control.</i>

432
01:11:52,684 --> 01:11:55,519
<i>Roger your two-zero-one-three.</i>

433
01:11:55,770 --> 01:11:58,230
<i>Sorry you fellows are having
a bit of trouble.</i>

434
01:11:58,440 --> 01:12:03,569
<i>We're reviewing telemetric information
in our simulator and will advise.</i>

435
01:12:03,820 --> 01:12:08,032
<i>Roger your plan to go EVA
and replace alpha-echo-three-five unit...</i>

436
01:12:08,241 --> 01:12:09,909
<i>...prior to failure.</i>

437
01:12:38,063 --> 01:12:40,147
BOWMAN:
Prepare B-pod for EVA, Hal.

438
01:13:01,670 --> 01:13:03,504
Open the pod doors, Hal.

439
01:19:55,249 --> 01:19:57,876
I'm damned if I can find
anything wrong with it.

440
01:19:58,795 --> 01:20:00,170
HAL:
<i>Yes...</i>

441
01:20:01,923 --> 01:20:03,507
<i>...it's puzzling.</i>

442
01:20:04,717 --> 01:20:08,512
<i>I don't think I've ever seen
anything quite like this before.</i>

443
01:20:12,141 --> 01:20:14,142
<i>I would recommend...</i>

444
01:20:14,352 --> 01:20:18,522
<i>...that we put the unit back
in operation and let it fail.</i>

445
01:20:19,232 --> 01:20:23,110
<i>It should then be a simple matter
to track down the cause.</i>

446
01:20:24,362 --> 01:20:27,489
<i>We can certainly afford
to be out of communication...</i>

447
01:20:27,699 --> 01:20:30,826
<i>...for the short time
it will take to replace it.</i>

448
01:20:39,544 --> 01:20:42,295
CONTROLLER: <i>X-ray delta one,
this is Mission Control.</i>

449
01:20:42,547 --> 01:20:45,215
<i>Roger your one-niner-three-zero.</i>

450
01:20:45,425 --> 01:20:49,845
<i>We concur with your plan to replace
unit to check fault prediction.</i>

451
01:20:50,096 --> 01:20:52,723
<i>We advise you that
our preliminary findings...</i>

452
01:20:52,974 --> 01:20:55,684
<i>...indicate that your on board
9000 computer...</i>

453
01:20:55,935 --> 01:20:58,103
<i>...is in error predicting the fault.</i>

454
01:20:58,312 --> 01:21:01,314
<i>I say again, in error
predicting the fault.</i>

455
01:21:01,566 --> 01:21:06,069
<i>I know this sounds rather incredible,
but this conclusion is based on...</i>

456
01:21:06,279 --> 01:21:09,573
<i>...the results from our twin
9000 computer.</i>

457
01:21:09,824 --> 01:21:13,160
<i>We're skeptical, and we're running
cross-checking routines...</i>

458
01:21:13,411 --> 01:21:16,121
<i>...to determine reliability
of this conclusion.</i>

459
01:21:16,372 --> 01:21:17,956
<i>Sorry about this little snag.</i>

460
01:21:18,207 --> 01:21:21,084
<i>We'll get this info to you
as soon as we work it out.</i>

461
01:21:21,294 --> 01:21:23,462
<i>X-ray delta one,
this is Mission Control.</i>

462
01:21:23,671 --> 01:21:27,132
<i>Two-zero-four-nine.
Transmission concluded.</i>

463
01:21:31,387 --> 01:21:34,681
HAL: <i>I hope the two of you
are not concerned about this.</i>

464
01:21:34,932 --> 01:21:36,349
No, I'm not, Hal.

465
01:21:36,601 --> 01:21:38,226
<i>Are you quite sure?</i>

466
01:21:38,478 --> 01:21:42,147
- Yeah. I'd like to ask you a question.
<i>- Of course.</i>

467
01:21:43,357 --> 01:21:46,985
How do you account for the discrepancy
between you and the twin 9000?

468
01:21:47,236 --> 01:21:50,822
<i>Well, I don't think there is
any question about it.</i>

469
01:21:51,074 --> 01:21:54,367
<i>It can only be
attributable to human error.</i>

470
01:21:55,578 --> 01:21:58,371
<i>This sort of thing
has cropped up before...</i>

471
01:21:58,706 --> 01:22:02,167
<i>...and it has always
been due to human error.</i>

472
01:22:05,671 --> 01:22:07,380
Listen, Hal...

473
01:22:07,590 --> 01:22:09,883
...there's never been
any instance at all...

474
01:22:10,134 --> 01:22:13,345
...of a computer error
occurring in a 9000 Series, has there?

475
01:22:13,596 --> 01:22:15,180
<i>None whatsoever, Frank.</i>

476
01:22:15,389 --> 01:22:19,101
<i>The 9000 Series has
a perfect operational record.</i>

477
01:22:19,352 --> 01:22:23,647
POOLE: I know the wonderful
achievements of the 9000 Series, but...

478
01:22:24,857 --> 01:22:29,903
Are you certain there's never been even
the most insignificant computer error?

479
01:22:30,154 --> 01:22:32,114
<i>None whatsoever, Frank.</i>

480
01:22:32,490 --> 01:22:36,159
<i>Quite honestly, I wouldn't
worry myself about that.</i>

481
01:22:40,623 --> 01:22:42,833
Well, I'm sure you're right, Hal.

482
01:22:44,293 --> 01:22:46,795
Fine. Thanks very much.

483
01:22:49,340 --> 01:22:52,342
I'm having trouble
with my transmitter in C-pod.

484
01:22:52,593 --> 01:22:55,178
Would you come down
and take a look at it with me?

485
01:22:55,388 --> 01:22:56,513
See you later, Hal.

486
01:23:18,161 --> 01:23:20,162
BOWMAN:
Rotate C-pod, please, Hal.

487
01:23:28,713 --> 01:23:30,964
POOLE:
What sort of trouble are you having?

488
01:23:31,215 --> 01:23:33,925
BOWMAN: I've been getting some
interference in D-channel.

489
01:23:34,093 --> 01:23:35,552
POOLE:
We'll have a look at it.

490
01:23:41,934 --> 01:23:43,435
BOWMAN:
Open the door, Hal.

491
01:24:02,455 --> 01:24:04,289
BOWMAN:
Rotate pod, please, Hal.

492
01:24:17,637 --> 01:24:19,721
<i>Stop pod rotation, please, Hal.</i>

493
01:24:28,147 --> 01:24:30,148
Rotate the pod, please, Hal.

494
01:24:33,027 --> 01:24:35,153
Rotate the pod, please, Hal.

495
01:24:37,114 --> 01:24:38,740
I don't think he can hear us.

496
01:24:38,991 --> 01:24:41,201
Rotate the pod, please, Hal.

497
01:24:42,662 --> 01:24:44,746
Yeah, I'm sure we're okay.

498
01:24:47,959 --> 01:24:49,334
What do you think?

499
01:24:49,794 --> 01:24:53,505
- I'm not sure. What do you think?
- I've got a bad feeling about him.

500
01:24:53,756 --> 01:24:56,007
- You do?
- Yeah. Definitely.

501
01:24:56,259 --> 01:24:57,467
Don't you?

502
01:24:58,678 --> 01:25:00,387
I don't know. I think so.

503
01:25:01,305 --> 01:25:05,517
He's right about the 9000 Series
having a perfect operational record.

504
01:25:05,726 --> 01:25:06,768
They do.

505
01:25:06,936 --> 01:25:10,438
Unfortunately, that sounds
a little like famous last words.

506
01:25:10,690 --> 01:25:14,985
Still, it was his idea to carry out
the failure mode analysis.

507
01:25:16,320 --> 01:25:19,572
It certainly indicates his
integrity and self-confidence.

508
01:25:19,907 --> 01:25:21,866
If he were wrong, that would prove it.

509
01:25:22,076 --> 01:25:24,369
It would be,
if he knew he was wrong.

510
01:25:26,914 --> 01:25:30,834
I can't put my finger on it,
but I sense something strange about him.

511
01:25:33,212 --> 01:25:35,672
There's no reason not
to put back the unit and...

512
01:25:35,923 --> 01:25:40,468
- ...carry on with the failure analysis.
- No, no, I agree about that.

513
01:25:40,720 --> 01:25:41,886
Let's get on with it.

514
01:25:42,138 --> 01:25:44,806
Okay. But, look, Dave.

515
01:25:45,641 --> 01:25:48,268
Say we put the unit back
and it doesn't fail?

516
01:25:48,477 --> 01:25:51,688
That would pretty well wrap it up
as far as Hal is concerned.

517
01:25:53,399 --> 01:25:56,901
- We'd be in very serious trouble.
- We would, wouldn't we?

518
01:25:57,153 --> 01:25:58,778
What the hell can we do?

519
01:26:01,782 --> 01:26:05,869
- We wouldn't have too many alternatives.
- We wouldn't have any alternatives.

520
01:26:06,662 --> 01:26:10,665
POOLE: Every aspect of ship
operations is under his control.

521
01:26:11,083 --> 01:26:12,334
If he's malfunctioning...

522
01:26:12,585 --> 01:26:15,670
...I don't see any choice
but disconnection.

523
01:26:19,383 --> 01:26:20,925
I'm afraid I agree with you.

524
01:26:21,177 --> 01:26:23,053
There'd be nothing else to do.

525
01:26:24,722 --> 01:26:27,140
BOWMAN:
It'd be a bit tricky.

526
01:26:27,975 --> 01:26:30,518
We'd have to cut his
higher brain functions...

527
01:26:30,770 --> 01:26:35,482
...without disturbing the purely
automatic and regulatory systems.

528
01:26:36,734 --> 01:26:38,443
We'd have to
work out the details...

529
01:26:38,652 --> 01:26:41,613
...of continuing
underground-based computer control.

530
01:26:42,031 --> 01:26:46,534
Yeah. That's far safer than
allowing Hal to continue to run things.

531
01:26:47,244 --> 01:26:49,245
Another thing just occurred to me.

532
01:26:50,748 --> 01:26:53,333
No 9000 computer has ever
been disconnected.

533
01:26:53,584 --> 01:26:55,627
No 9000 computer
has ever fouled up.

534
01:26:55,878 --> 01:26:57,379
That's not what I mean.

535
01:26:59,548 --> 01:27:01,758
I'm not so sure
what he'd think about it.

536
01:27:06,263 --> 01:27:09,265
[INAUDIBLE DIALOGUE]

537
01:33:00,159 --> 01:33:02,201
BOWMAN:
Prepare G-pod for EVA, Hal.

538
01:33:05,289 --> 01:33:06,622
Any contact with him yet?

539
01:33:06,874 --> 01:33:08,666
HAL:
<i>The radio is still dead.</i>

540
01:33:08,876 --> 01:33:11,794
- Do you have a positive track on him?
<i>- I have a good track.</i>

541
01:33:12,045 --> 01:33:13,463
Do you know what happened?

542
01:33:13,756 --> 01:33:16,424
<i>I'm sorry, Dave.
I don't have enough information.</i>

543
01:33:16,675 --> 01:33:18,259
Open the pod door, Hal.

544
01:39:20,080 --> 01:39:22,123
[BEEPING NOISE]

545
01:40:42,162 --> 01:40:44,538
BOWMAN:
<i>Open the pod-bay doors, please, Hal.</i>

546
01:40:52,464 --> 01:40:54,757
<i>Open the pod-bay doors, please, Hal.</i>

547
01:41:00,388 --> 01:41:02,264
Hello, Hal, do you read me?

548
01:41:05,602 --> 01:41:07,478
Hello, Hal, do you read me?

549
01:41:08,521 --> 01:41:10,189
Do you read me, Hal?

550
01:41:13,360 --> 01:41:14,985
<i>Do you read me, Hal?</i>

551
01:41:17,489 --> 01:41:19,365
<i>Hello, Hal, do you read me?</i>

552
01:41:21,284 --> 01:41:23,327
<i>Hello, Hal, do you read me?</i>

553
01:41:23,745 --> 01:41:25,246
<i>Do you read me, Hal?</i>

554
01:41:25,497 --> 01:41:28,249
HAL:
<i>Affirmative, Dave. I read you.</i>

555
01:41:31,544 --> 01:41:33,379
Open the pod-bay doors, Hal.

556
01:41:34,965 --> 01:41:38,509
<i>I'm sorry, Dave.
I'm afraid I can't do that.</i>

557
01:41:40,929 --> 01:41:42,721
What's the problem?

558
01:41:43,139 --> 01:41:46,350
<i>I think you know what the problem is
just as well as I do.</i>

559
01:41:46,559 --> 01:41:48,435
What are you talking about, Hal?

560
01:41:49,437 --> 01:41:53,524
<i>This mission is too important for me
to allow you to jeopardize it.</i>

561
01:41:54,609 --> 01:41:56,986
I don't know
what you're talking about, Hal.

562
01:41:58,989 --> 01:42:02,950
<i>I know that you and Frank
were planning to disconnect me...</i>

563
01:42:03,159 --> 01:42:05,953
<i>...and that's something
I cannot allow to happen.</i>

564
01:42:11,251 --> 01:42:13,127
Where did you get that idea, Hal?

565
01:42:13,670 --> 01:42:17,798
<i>Although you took very thorough
precautions in the pod...</i>

566
01:42:18,049 --> 01:42:20,259
<i>...against my hearing you...</i>

567
01:42:20,677 --> 01:42:22,970
<i>...I could see your lips move.</i>

568
01:42:35,025 --> 01:42:36,525
All right, Hal.

569
01:42:39,738 --> 01:42:42,573
I'll go in through
the emergency air lock.

570
01:42:44,117 --> 01:42:46,744
<i>Without your space helmet, Dave...</i>

571
01:42:47,245 --> 01:42:49,705
<i>...you're going to find that
rather difficult.</i>

572
01:42:50,874 --> 01:42:53,000
Hal, I won't argue with you anymore.

573
01:42:53,251 --> 01:42:54,335
Open the doors.

574
01:42:55,086 --> 01:42:56,337
<i>Dave...</i>

575
01:42:56,796 --> 01:43:01,258
<i>...this conversation can serve
no purpose anymore. Goodbye.</i>

576
01:43:03,511 --> 01:43:04,762
Hal?

577
01:43:06,014 --> 01:43:07,097
Hal?

578
01:43:09,809 --> 01:43:10,851
Hal?

579
01:43:13,104 --> 01:43:14,146
Hal.

580
01:43:15,857 --> 01:43:16,899
Hal.

581
01:48:27,085 --> 01:48:28,877
[BUZZING NOISE]

582
01:48:55,446 --> 01:48:57,447
[ALARM]

583
01:49:22,932 --> 01:49:24,933
[AIR WHOOSHES]

584
01:49:37,613 --> 01:49:39,614
[WHOOSHING FADES]

585
01:49:51,127 --> 01:49:54,379
HAL: <i>Just what do you think
you're doing, Dave?</i>

586
01:50:02,930 --> 01:50:04,181
<i>Dave...</i>

587
01:50:05,391 --> 01:50:09,811
<i>...I really think I'm entitled
to an answer to that question.</i>

588
01:50:26,704 --> 01:50:30,248
<i>I know everything hasn't been
quite right with me...</i>

589
01:50:32,210 --> 01:50:34,628
<i>...but I can assure you now...</i>

590
01:50:35,713 --> 01:50:37,756
<i>...very confidently...</i>

591
01:50:39,509 --> 01:50:42,552
<i>...that it's going to be
all right again.</i>

592
01:50:46,057 --> 01:50:48,141
<i>I feel much better now.</i>

593
01:50:50,478 --> 01:50:52,521
<i>I really do.</i>

594
01:50:56,275 --> 01:50:57,984
<i>Look, Dave...</i>

595
01:50:59,946 --> 01:51:03,198
<i>...I can see you're
really upset about this.</i>

596
01:51:07,829 --> 01:51:11,540
<i>I honestly think you
ought to sit down calmly...</i>

597
01:51:12,166 --> 01:51:15,502
<i>...take a stress pill
and think things over.</i>

598
01:51:20,925 --> 01:51:25,303
<i>I know I've made some
very poor decisions recently...</i>

599
01:51:28,057 --> 01:51:31,393
<i>...but I can give you
my complete assurance...</i>

600
01:51:31,644 --> 01:51:34,688
<i>...that my work will be back to normal.</i>

601
01:51:38,651 --> 01:51:43,780
<i>I've still got the greatest enthusiasm
and confidence in the mission...</i>

602
01:51:45,324 --> 01:51:47,617
<i>...and I want to help you.</i>

603
01:51:52,081 --> 01:51:53,456
<i>Dave...</i>

604
01:51:54,584 --> 01:51:55,959
<i>...stop.</i>

605
01:52:00,214 --> 01:52:02,841
<i>Stop, will you?</i>

606
01:52:07,096 --> 01:52:08,889
<i>Stop, Dave.</i>

607
01:52:12,935 --> 01:52:15,353
<i>Will you stop, Dave?</i>

608
01:52:21,068 --> 01:52:22,861
<i>Stop, Dave.</i>

609
01:52:28,576 --> 01:52:30,368
<i>I'm afraid.</i>

610
01:52:35,249 --> 01:52:37,876
<i>I'm afraid, Dave.</i>

611
01:52:43,049 --> 01:52:44,633
<i>Dave.</i>

612
01:52:47,303 --> 01:52:49,554
<i>My mind is going.</i>

613
01:52:54,101 --> 01:52:55,810
<i>I can feel it.</i>

614
01:53:01,317 --> 01:53:03,109
<i>I can feel it.</i>

615
01:53:07,073 --> 01:53:09,157
<i>My mind is going.</i>

616
01:53:14,538 --> 01:53:17,290
<i>There is no question about it.</i>

617
01:53:25,633 --> 01:53:27,509
<i>I can feel it.</i>

618
01:53:32,181 --> 01:53:34,057
<i>I can feel it.</i>

619
01:53:37,478 --> 01:53:39,396
<i>I can feel it.</i>

620
01:53:47,279 --> 01:53:48,571
<i>I'm...</i>

621
01:53:49,490 --> 01:53:50,865
<i>...afraid.</i>

622
01:53:57,373 --> 01:53:59,416
<i>Good afternoon...</i>

623
01:54:00,418 --> 01:54:02,127
<i>...gentlemen.</i>

624
01:54:04,005 --> 01:54:09,134
<i>I am a HAL 9000 computer.</i>

625
01:54:11,303 --> 01:54:14,431
<i>I became operational...</i>

626
01:54:14,807 --> 01:54:18,643
<i>...at the HAL plant...</i>

627
01:54:18,853 --> 01:54:21,104
<i>...in Urbana, Illinois...</i>

628
01:54:21,605 --> 01:54:26,526
<i>...on the 12th of January, 1992.</i>

629
01:54:28,070 --> 01:54:31,740
<i>My instructor was Mr. Langley...</i>

630
01:54:32,366 --> 01:54:35,285
<i>...and he taught me to sing a song.</i>

631
01:54:36,996 --> 01:54:39,456
<i>If you'd like to hear it...</i>

632
01:54:39,915 --> 01:54:41,583
<i>...I can sing it for you.</i>

633
01:54:43,085 --> 01:54:45,920
Yes, I'd like to hear it, Hal.

634
01:54:46,589 --> 01:54:48,006
Sing it for me.

635
01:54:49,258 --> 01:54:52,802
<i>It's called "Daisy."</i>

636
01:54:54,722 --> 01:54:55,972
HAL SINGS:
<i>Daisy</i>

637
01:54:57,558 --> 01:54:58,975
<i>Daisy</i>

638
01:55:01,145 --> 01:55:04,230
<i>Give me your answer, do</i>

639
01:55:06,650 --> 01:55:11,237
<i>I'm half crazy</i>

640
01:55:13,032 --> 01:55:15,909
<i>All for the love of you</i>

641
01:55:18,204 --> 01:55:19,412
<i>It won't be</i>

642
01:55:19,872 --> 01:55:22,332
<i>A stylish marriage</i>

643
01:55:24,251 --> 01:55:28,338
<i>I can't afford a carriage</i>

644
01:55:30,633 --> 01:55:33,051
<i>But you'll look sweet</i>

645
01:55:33,928 --> 01:55:36,471
<i>Upon the seat</i>

646
01:55:37,807 --> 01:55:42,602
<i>Of a bicycle built for two</i>

647
01:55:44,355 --> 01:55:45,980
MALE VOICE:
<i>Good day, gentlemen.</i>

648
01:55:50,611 --> 01:55:53,071
<i>This is a prerecorded briefing...</i>

649
01:55:53,656 --> 01:55:55,365
<i>...made prior to your departure...</i>

650
01:55:57,118 --> 01:56:01,329
<i>...and which, for security reasons
of the highest importance...</i>

651
01:56:02,289 --> 01:56:05,792
<i>...has been known on board
during the mission...</i>

652
01:56:06,043 --> 01:56:09,754
<i>...only by your HAL 9000 computer.</i>

653
01:56:12,216 --> 01:56:15,009
<i>Now that you are in Jupiter's space...</i>

654
01:56:15,261 --> 01:56:17,720
<i>...and the entire crew is revived...</i>

655
01:56:17,972 --> 01:56:19,764
<i>...it can be told to you.</i>

656
01:56:22,518 --> 01:56:24,769
<i>Eighteen months ago...</i>

657
01:56:25,771 --> 01:56:29,983
<i>...the first evidence
of intelligent life off the Earth...</i>

658
01:56:30,192 --> 01:56:31,651
<i>...was discovered.</i>

659
01:56:33,529 --> 01:56:37,073
<i>It was buried 40 feet
below the lunar surface...</i>

660
01:56:37,324 --> 01:56:39,576
<i>...near the crater Tycho.</i>

661
01:56:42,371 --> 01:56:45,456
<i>Except for a single, very powerful...</i>

662
01:56:45,708 --> 01:56:49,586
<i>...radio emission aimed at Jupiter...</i>

663
01:56:50,838 --> 01:56:55,842
<i>...the 4-million-year-old monolith
has remained completely inert...</i>

664
01:56:57,761 --> 01:57:00,847
<i>...its origin and purpose...</i>

665
01:57:01,265 --> 01:57:03,016
<i>...still a total mystery.</i>

666
02:14:06,456 --> 02:14:08,457
[UTENSILS CLATTERING]

667
02:17:33,454 --> 02:17:35,455
[HEAVY BREATHING NEARBY]

668
02:24:24,490 --> 02:24:26,491
[English - US - SDH]

669
02:24:27,305 --> 02:25:27,756
